The slice-1 gate test (internal/integration/encounter_v2_test.go) is the canonical pattern for v2 integration tests. To add a new test:

  type MyV2TestSuite struct {
      suite.Suite
      ctx context.Context
      srv *harness.TestServer
  }

  func (s *MyV2TestSuite) SetupTest() {
      s.ctx = context.Background()
      var err error
      s.srv, err = harness.New(s.ctx, nil)
      s.Require().NoError(err)
  }

  func (s *MyV2TestSuite) TearDownTest() { s.srv.Close() }

Seed an encounter directly through the toolkit + repo — this avoids needing CreateEncounter to exist:

  enc := tkenc.New("enc-1", s.srv.BrokerV2)
  // ... configure players, geometry, entities via toolkit verbs
  s.Require().NoError(s.srv.EncRepoV2.Save(s.ctx, enc.ToData()))

Then exercise via s.srv.EncounterClientV2 (a real gRPC client over bufconn). Stream + RPC calls go through the registered v2 handler, broker projection runs end-to-end. authCtx(playerID) helper attaches the Authorization: Dev <playerID> header.

Once CreateEncounter exists (rpg-api#499), tests can also create through the RPC — but the direct-construct path stays valid for fixture setup.

Shipped in Wave 2.8 (rpg-api PR #507). Mode-aware handlers must check the encounter mode before dispatching the verb:

  if enc.Mode() != core.EncounterModeTurnBased {
      return nil, status.Error(codes.FailedPrecondition, "encounter is not in TURN_BASED mode")
  }

Applies to: TakeAction, EndTurn, and any future RPC whose semantics only make sense in initiative-based combat. MoveEntity stays mode-agnostic (free-roam movement is valid).

Why FailedPrecondition (not InvalidArgument): the request is syntactically valid; world state forbids it. Same convention as pat-v2-status-code-mapping.

When adding a new turn-based-only RPC: include the mode check at the TOP of the handler, before any other validation that could produce a confusing error. Test with both modes (FREE_ROAM + TURN_BASED) in the handler suite — see encounter_v2/take_action_test.go for the template.

Shipped in Wave 2.10 (rpg-api PR #517 + rpg-toolkit PR #643). Encounter end is server-authoritative terminal state held by the toolkit aggregate (`enc.Mode() == core.ModeEnded`). Every state-mutating verb on the v2 handler (`MoveEntity`, `EndTurn`, `TakeAction`, `Interact`, `SubmitCheck`) MUST check terminal state first — load encounter, then:

  if enc.Mode() == core.ModeEnded {
      return nil, status.Error(codes.FailedPrecondition, "encounter has ended")
  }

The toolkit returns `ErrEncounterEnded` when the verb itself is invoked on a terminal encounter; the handler maps it via pat-v2-status-code-mapping. Web client should never see a successful response for a verb against an ended encounter.

`EncounterEndedEvent` is published exactly ONCE — when the predicate first goes true (see toolkit pat-encounter-end-predicate). Subsequent inspections of the encounter (live verbs returning ErrEncounterEnded; snapshot replay for late-joiners reflecting terminal mode) do NOT re-publish the event. This keeps event-stream consumers from seeing duplicates on reconnect.

Late-joiner contract: a client connecting to an ended encounter via StreamEncounter receives the SnapshotDelivered with the terminal-mode projection (see pat-v2-mode-ended-wire-mapping for how that's expressed on the wire). It does NOT receive a synthetic EncounterEndedEvent in the replay — the snapshot is the source of truth for already-ended state.

When adding a new state-mutating verb in future waves: add the terminal-state guard at the TOP of the handler, before any other validation that could produce a confusing error. Mode-gated verbs (TakeAction, EndTurn) check terminal state BEFORE the TURN_BASED check (see pat-v2-mode-gating); a terminal encounter is no longer in any normal mode.

Reference rpg-api#517 and the toolkit's pat-encounter-terminal-mode for the encapsulation.

Shipped in Wave 2.10 (rpg-api PR #517). The EndTurn handler's NPC cycle (see pat-v2-npc-turn-dispatch from Wave 2.8) added a terminal-state check after each `enc.NPCAct(active)` / `enc.EndTurn(active)` iteration:

  for i := 0; i < len(enc.InitiativeOrder()) + 4; i++ {
      if enc.Mode() == core.ModeEnded {
          break  // encounter ended mid-NPC-chain; stop dispatching
      }
      active := enc.ActiveActor()
      if active is a player → break
      if active is an NPC → enc.NPCAct(active)
  }

Why: in Wave 2.10, `killEntity` only runs in `TakeAction`'s player-attack path (player kills monster). The NPC path (NPCAct → goblin attacks alice) does NOT call killEntity for a player kill — players don't get removed on death (that's Wave 2.11+ death-saves territory). So today this guard never fires.

But forward-loading: future waves will introduce factions (NPC-on-NPC damage) and player-killable NPCs in the NPC turn (an NPC might damage another NPC who is at 1 HP and trigger killEntity → checkEncounterEnd → ModeEnded). Without this guard, the NPC loop would continue dispatching to actors that no longer exist or to a terminal encounter. Cheap insurance.

The guard fires BEFORE `enc.ActiveActor()` because if the previous NPC's action ended the encounter, the active actor pointer may be stale (initiative spliced, ActiveIdx adjusted). Reading mode is the cleaner single check.

When extending the cycle (reactions, opportunity attacks, delayed actions in future waves): keep the terminal-state check at the TOP of every iteration. It's the canonical "can I keep dispatching?" question.
